From: Steam-Link-PS5
Subject: retransmission: resend on NACK, back off instead of 21 sends in 200 ms

A NACK from the host names a reliable packet it is missing, but the session
handled it like an ACK and cancelled the retransmission. Combined with the
fixed schedule (retry every 10 ms, give up after 20 tries) one lost packet on a
Wi-Fi link wedged the host's control channel for the rest of the session:
every later packet (keyframe requests, input, stats) was NACKed and the client
had long forgotten the one the host wanted. Measured on a PS5 over Wi-Fi: 263
distinct control packets in 6 s went out 4802 times, the host answered with
1677 NACKs all naming the same id, and the video froze because no keyframe
request ever got through.

Also make IHS_QueuePollBy detach the polled node: it left the next pointer
dangling, and re-appending such a node built a cycle the send thread walked
forever.

Resend the named packet on NACK (rate limited to one per 30 ms), keep pending
packets for about 17 s with retries backing off from 60 ms to 1 s, and warn
when the host asks for a packet that is no longer pending.

